---
import ToolPage from '../../components/ToolPage.astro';
import AfterTaxCalc from '../../components/calc/AfterTaxCalc';
import StateTables from '../../components/StateTables.astro';
import { FUNDS, fund, pct, day, META, YIELD_DAYS } from '../../lib/data';
import { fiftyTest, stateRate } from '../../lib/engine/tax';
import { src, related, METHOD, PAGE_FUNDS, exampleVerdict } from '../../lib/common';
import { title as fitTitle, desc as fitDesc } from '../../lib/snippet';
const govs = FUNDS.filter((f) => ['government', 'treasury', 'treasury-only'].includes(f.k));
const fails = govs.filter((f) => fiftyTest(f.q) === 'fails');
const caMunis = FUNDS.filter((f) => f.k === 'state-muni' && f.st === 'CA');
const vct = fund('VCTXX');
const r93 = stateRate({ state: 'CA', filing: 'single', income: 120000 });
const title = fitTitle(['California Money Market Fund Tax: The 50% Rule by Fund', 'California Money Market Fund Tax: The 50% Rule, Fund by Fund']);
const description = fitDesc([
  `California money market fund tax: which funds passed the 50% quarter-end test on their filings, and what each yield leaves after California and federal tax.`,
  `California money market fund tax: which funds passed the 50% quarter-end test in their filings, and what each fund’s yield leaves you after California and federal tax.`,
]);
const faqs = [
  { q: 'Are money market fund dividends taxable in California?', a: `Yes, unless they come from U.S. or California government debt held by a fund that kept at least 50% of its assets in such debt at the close of each quarter of its tax year. Then that part of the dividend is not taxed, and you subtract it on your California return.` },
  { q: 'Is a California municipal money fund better than a Treasury fund?', a: `It depends on your income. On the latest yields VCTXX paid ${pct(vct.y)} free of both federal and California tax, while VUSXX paid ${pct(fund('VUSXX').y)} free of California tax only. The calculator above compares the two for your income; at most incomes on these yields the Treasury fund kept more.` },
  { q: 'Where do I find the tax-free percentage for California?', a: `In the fund company’s year-end tax information, often a table titled U.S. government obligations or state tax information. Many companies also say there whether the fund met California’s 50% requirement for the year. Use that figure, not this site’s estimate, on your return.` },
];
---
<ToolPage id="california" title={title} description={description} crumb="California"
  h1="California money market fund tax: the 50% rule, fund by fund"
  lede={`California money market fund tax: California does not tax what a fund earns on U.S. government debt, if at least half the fund was in that debt at every quarter end. ${exampleVerdict(fund('VUSXX'), vct)} VUSXX holds U.S. Treasury debt. VCTXX lends to California and its cities, and pays no federal or state tax. Not tax or investment advice.`}
  faqs={faqs} related={related('fifty-percent-rule', 'fund-vctxx', 'vmfxx-vs-vusxx')} sources={src('ftb-ca', 'ftb-ca-rates', 'nmfp', 'irs-rp')}>
  <AfterTaxCalc client:load funds={PAGE_FUNDS} a="VUSXX" b="VCTXX" methodHref={METHOD} defState="CA" id="c-ca" />
  <div class="prose prose-slate mt-10 max-w-none">
    <h2>What California exempts from tax on money funds, and when</h2>
    <p>Miss the 50% line at one quarter end, and the whole year’s dividend is taxed, even for a fund with Treasury in its name. In the data, {fails.length} of the {govs.length} government and Treasury versions of funds were below 50% at the June 30 or September 30 quarter end. What counts toward the 50%: Treasury bills and notes, and debt of the Federal Home Loan Banks and Farm Credit Banks. What does not: short loans to banks backed by Treasuries (called repo), and debt of Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac. The other route is a California municipal fund: lower yield, but no federal or California tax for residents. The data holds {caMunis.length} of them; the calculator above sets one against the Treasury fund VUSXX at your income.</p>
  </div>
  <StateTables st="CA" />
</ToolPage>
